Article 27 In any of the following circumstances, a resolution of the shareholders' meeting or the board of directors of a company shall not be established:
(1) A resolution was made without convening a meeting of the shareholders' meeting or the board of directors;
(2) The shareholders' meeting or the board of directors meeting did not vote on the matter of the resolution;
(3) The number of persons present at the meeting or the number of voting rights held did not meet the requirements of this Law or the company's articles of association;
(4) The number of persons who voted in favor of the resolution or the number of voting rights held did not meet the requirements of this Law or the company's articles of association.

Article 32 The registration items of a company include:
(1) Name;
(2) Domicile;
(3) Registered capital;
(4) Business scope;
(5) Name of the legal representative;
(6) Names or titles of the shareholders of a limited liability company and the promoters of a joint stock limited company.
The company registration authority shall publicly disclose the aforesaid registration items of the company through the National Enterprise Credit Information Publicity System.

Article 40 A company shall, in accordance with regulations, publicly disclose the following items through the National Enterprise Credit Information Publicity System:
(1) The amount, method, and date of capital contributions subscribed and paid by shareholders of a limited liability company, and the number of shares subscribed by promoters of a joint stock limited company;
(2) Information on changes in equity or shares of shareholders of a limited liability company and promoters of a joint stock limited company;
(3) Information on the acquisition, change, and cancellation of administrative licenses;
(4) Other information as provided by laws and administrative regulations.
A company shall ensure that the information disclosed in the preceding paragraph is authentic, accurate, and complete.

Article 46 The articles of association of a limited liability company shall specify the following:
(1) Name and domicile of the company;
(2) Business scope of the company;
(3) Registered capital of the company;
(4) Names or titles of the shareholders;
(5) Amount, method and date of capital contribution of each shareholder;
(6) The company's organs and their formation methods, functions, and rules of procedure;
(7) Methods for the appointment and change of the company's legal representative;
(8) Other matters deemed necessary by the shareholders' meeting.
The shareholders shall sign or affix their seals to the company's articles of association.

Article 55 After the establishment of a limited liability company, it shall issue a capital contribution certificate to each shareholder, which shall specify the following:
(1) Name of the company;
(2) Date of establishment of the company;
(3) Registered capital of the company;
(4) Names or titles of the shareholders, the amount of capital contribution subscribed and actually paid, the method of contribution, and the date of contribution;
(5) Serial number and date of issuance of the capital contribution certificate.
The capital contribution certificate shall be signed by the legal representative and affixed with the company's seal.

Article 56 A limited liability company shall maintain a register of shareholders, which shall record the following:
(1) Names or titles and addresses of the shareholders;
(2) Amount of capital contribution subscribed and actually paid, method of contribution, and date of contribution of each shareholder;
(3) Serial numbers of capital contribution certificates;
(4) Dates of acquisition and loss of shareholder status.
Shareholders recorded in the register of shareholders may claim and exercise their shareholder rights in accordance with the register.

Article 59 The shareholders' meeting shall exercise the following functions and powers:
(1) Elect and replace directors and supervisors, and decide on matters relating to the remuneration of directors and supervisors;
(2) Deliberate and approve reports of the board of directors;
(3) Deliberate and approve reports of the board of supervisors;
(4) Deliberate and approve the company's profit distribution plan and plan for making up losses;
(5) Adopt resolutions on increasing or reducing the registered capital of the company;
(6) Adopt resolutions on the issuance of corporate bonds;
(7) Adopt resolutions on the merger, division, dissolution, liquidation, or change of the company form;
(8) Amend the company's articles of association;
(9) Other functions and powers as specified in the company's articles of association.
The shareholders' meeting may authorize the board of directors to adopt resolutions on the issuance of corporate bonds.
If all shareholders unanimously agree in writing on the matters listed in the first paragraph of this Article, the shareholders' meeting may not be convened and a decision may be made directly, and the decision document shall be signed or sealed by all shareholders.

Article 67 A limited liability company shall have a board of directors, except as otherwise provided in Article 75 of this Law.
The board of directors shall exercise the following functions and powers:
(1) to convene shareholders' meetings and report on its work to the shareholders' meeting;
(2) to execute the resolutions of the shareholders' meeting;
(3) to decide on the company's business plans and investment schemes;
(4) to formulate the company's profit distribution plan and loss recovery plan;
(5) to formulate plans for increasing or reducing the company's registered capital and for issuing corporate bonds;
(6) to formulate plans for the merger, division, dissolution, or change of the company form;
(7) to decide on the setting up of the company's internal management structure;
(8) to decide on the appointment or dismissal of the company's manager and his remuneration, and, upon the manager's recommendation, to decide on the appointment or dismissal of the deputy manager and the financial officer of the company and their remuneration;
(9) to formulate the company's basic management system;
(10) other functions and powers as provided by the company's articles of association or granted by the shareholders' meeting.
The restriction on the functions and powers of the board of directors provided in the company's articles of association shall not be set up against a bona fide third party.

Article 78 The board of supervisors shall exercise the following functions and powers:
(1) to inspect the company's financial affairs;
(2) to supervise the conduct of directors and senior officers in performing their duties, and to propose the removal of any director or senior officer who violates laws, administrative regulations, the company's articles of association, or a resolution of the shareholders' meeting;
(3) to demand that directors and senior officers correct any conduct that harms the company's interests;
(4) to propose the convening of an interim shareholders' meeting, and to convene and preside over a shareholders' meeting when the board of directors fails to perform its duty of convening and presiding over such meeting as provided in this Law;
(5) to submit proposals to the shareholders' meeting;
(6) to initiate legal proceedings against directors and senior officers in accordance with Article 189 of this Law;
(7) other functions and powers as prescribed by the company's articles of association.

Article 95 The articles of association of a joint stock limited company shall contain the following matters:
(1) name and domicile of the company;
(2) business scope of the company;
(3) manner of establishment of the company;
(4) registered capital of the company, total number of issued shares and number of shares issued at incorporation, and the par value per share for par value shares;
(5) if shares of a class are issued, the number of shares of each class and the rights and obligations thereof;
(6) names or titles of the promoters, number of shares subscribed for, and method of capital contribution;
(7) composition, functions and powers, and rules of procedure of the board of directors;
(8) method for the appointment and change of the legal representative of the company;
(9) composition, functions and powers, and rules of procedure of the board of supervisors;
(10) method for distribution of profits of the company;
(11) grounds for dissolution of the company and liquidation method;
(12) method for notice and announcement of the company;
(13) other matters that the shareholders' meeting deems necessary to specify.

Article 102 A joint stock limited company shall prepare a register of shareholders and keep it at the company. The register of shareholders shall record the following matters:
(1) the names or titles and domiciles of the shareholders;
(2) the classes and numbers of shares subscribed for by each shareholder;
(3) if shares are issued in paper form, the serial numbers of the share certificates;
(4) the dates on which each shareholder acquired the shares.

Article 104 The inaugural meeting shall exercise the following functions and powers:
(1) to deliberate on the promoters' report on the preparatory matters of the company;
(2) to adopt the articles of association;
(3) to elect directors and supervisors;
(4) to examine the expenses incurred in the establishment of the company;
(5) to examine the valuation of non-monetary property contributed by the promoters;
(6) where force majeure or major changes in business conditions directly affect the establishment of the company, may resolve not to establish the company.
Resolutions of the inaugural meeting on the matters specified in the preceding paragraph shall be adopted by more than half of the voting rights held by the subscribers present at the meeting.

Article 113 The shareholders' meeting shall hold an annual meeting once a year. An extraordinary shareholders' meeting shall be convened within two months under any of the following circumstances:
(1) when the number of directors falls below the number prescribed by this Law or below two-thirds of the number set out in the company's articles of association;
(2) when the company's uncovered losses amount to one-third of the total share capital;
(3) upon the request of shareholders individually or jointly holding ten percent or more of the company's shares;
(4) when the board of directors deems it necessary;
(5) upon the proposal of the board of supervisors; or
(6) other circumstances as provided in the company's articles of association.

Article 137 Where a listed company sets up an audit committee within the board of directors, the following matters shall be approved by a majority of all members of the audit committee before the board of directors adopts a resolution:
(1) appointing or dismissing an accounting firm to conduct the company's audit;
(2) appointing or dismissing the chief financial officer;
(3) disclosing financial accounting reports;
(4) other matters as prescribed by the securities regulatory authority under the State Council.

Article 144 A company may, in accordance with the provisions of its articles of association, issue the following classes of shares with different rights from ordinary shares:
(1) shares with priority or subordination in distribution of profits or remaining property;
(2) shares with more or less voting rights per share than ordinary shares;
(3) shares with transfer restrictions, such as transfer subject to company consent;
(4) other classes of shares as prescribed by the State Council.
A company that publicly issues shares shall not issue the classes of shares specified in items (2) and (3) of the preceding paragraph, except for those already issued before public issuance.
Where a company issues the class of shares specified in item (2) of the first paragraph, the class shares and ordinary shares shall have the same voting rights per share with respect to the election and replacement of supervisors or members of the audit committee.

Article 145 A company that issues class shares shall specify the following matters in its articles of association:
(1) the order of distribution of profits or remaining property for class shares;
(2) the number of voting rights of class shares;
(3) transfer restrictions on class shares;
(4) measures to protect the rights and interests of minority shareholders;
(5) other matters deemed necessary to be specified by the shareholders' meeting.

Article 151 Where a company issues new shares, the shareholders' meeting shall make a resolution on the following matters:
(1) the class and number of new shares;
(2) the issue price of new shares;
(3) the commencement and closing dates for the issue of new shares;
(4) the class and number of new shares to be issued to existing shareholders;
(5) where shares without par value are issued, the amount of the proceeds from the issue of new shares to be included in the registered capital.
Where a company issues new shares, it may determine the pricing plan based on its business operations and financial condition.

Article 154 Where a company publicly offers shares to the public, it shall register with the securities regulatory authority under the State Council and publish a prospectus.
The prospectus shall be accompanied by the articles of association and shall specify the following:
(1) the total number of shares to be issued;
(2) the par value and issue price of shares with par value, or the issue price of shares without par value;
(3) the purpose of the funds raised;
(4) the rights and obligations of subscribers;
(5) the classes of shares and their rights and obligations;
(6) the commencement and closing dates of the offer and a statement that if the offer is not fully subscribed before the deadline, subscribers may withdraw their subscriptions.
Where shares are issued at the time of incorporation of the company, the number of shares subscribed by the promoters shall also be specified.

Article 161 Under any of the following circumstances, a shareholder who voted against the relevant resolution of the shareholders' meeting may request the company to purchase its shares at a reasonable price, unless the company is a company that has publicly issued shares:
(1) the company has not distributed profits to shareholders for five consecutive years, but has been profitable in those five consecutive years and meets the conditions for profit distribution prescribed by this Law;
(2) the company transfers its major assets;
(3) the business term specified in the articles of association expires or another ground for dissolution specified in the articles of association arises, and the shareholders' meeting adopts a resolution to amend the articles of association to continue the company.
If the shareholder and the company fail to reach an agreement on share purchase within sixty days from the date of the resolution of the shareholders' meeting, the shareholder may file a lawsuit with the People's Court within ninety days from the date of the resolution.
Shares of the company purchased by the company under the circumstances specified in paragraph 1 of this Article shall be transferred or cancelled according to law within six months.

Article 162 A company shall not purchase its own shares, except under any of the following circumstances:
(1) to reduce its registered capital;
(2) to merge with another company holding its shares;
(3) to use the shares for an employee share ownership plan or equity incentives;
(4) a shareholder requests the company to purchase his shares because he objects to a resolution of the shareholders' meeting on merger or division of the company;
(5) to use the shares for conversion of convertible bonds issued by the company;
(6) a listed company needs to do so to maintain its value and protect shareholders' rights.
Where the company purchases its own shares under circumstances specified in items (1) or (2) of the preceding paragraph, a resolution of the shareholders' meeting shall be required; where the company purchases its own shares under circumstances specified in items (3), (5), or (6) of the preceding paragraph, it may, in accordance with the articles of association or the authorization of the shareholders' meeting, adopt a resolution at a meeting of the board of directors attended by more than two-thirds of the directors.
After the company purchases its own shares in accordance with paragraph 1 of this Article, if it falls under item (1), the shares shall be cancelled within ten days from the date of purchase; if it falls under item (2) or (4), the shares shall be transferred or cancelled within six months; if it falls under item (3), (5), or (6), the total number of shares of the company held by the company shall not exceed ten percent of the total issued shares of the company and shall be transferred or cancelled within three years.
Where a listed company purchases its own shares, it shall fulfill its information disclosure obligations in accordance with the Securities Law of the People's Republic of China. Where a listed company purchases its own shares under circumstances specified in items (3), (5), or (6) of paragraph 1 of this Article, it shall conduct the purchase through public centralized trading.
A company shall not accept its own shares as the subject of a pledge.

Article 178 A person shall not serve as a director, supervisor, or senior officer of a company under any of the following circumstances:
(1) having no capacity for civil conduct or having limited capacity for civil conduct;
(2) having been sentenced to criminal punishment for embezzlement, bribery, misappropriation of property, or sabotage of the socialist market economic order, or having been deprived of political rights due to a crime, and less than five years have elapsed since the completion of the execution of the punishment; or, if a suspended sentence is imposed, less than two years have elapsed since the expiration of the probation period;
(3) having served as a director, factory director, or manager of a company or enterprise that went bankrupt due to liquidation, and bearing personal responsibility for the bankruptcy of such company or enterprise, and less than three years have elapsed since the completion of the bankruptcy liquidation of such company or enterprise;
(4) having served as the legal representative of a company or enterprise whose business license was revoked or which was ordered to close for illegal activities, and bearing personal responsibility therefor, and less than three years have elapsed since the date of such revocation or closure;
(5) being listed by a People's Court as a dishonest person subject to enforcement due to failure to pay off a relatively large amount of due debt.
Where a director, supervisor, or senior officer is elected, appointed, or engaged in violation of the preceding paragraph, such election, appointment, or engagement shall be invalid.
If a director, supervisor, or senior officer falls under any of the circumstances set forth in the first paragraph during their tenure of office, the company shall remove them from their position.

Article 181 Directors, supervisors, and senior officers shall not engage in any of the following acts:
(1) misappropriating company property or embezzling company funds;
(2) depositing company funds in accounts opened in their own name or in the name of other individuals;
(3) using their positions to solicit bribes or accept other illegal income;
(4) accepting commissions from third parties in transactions with the company and keeping them for themselves;
(5) disclosing company secrets without authorization;
(6) other acts in violation of the duty of loyalty to the company.

Article 183 Directors, supervisors, and senior officers shall not use their positions to seek business opportunities belonging to the company for themselves or others, except under any of the following circumstances:
(1) reporting to the board of directors or the shareholders' meeting and obtaining a resolution approved by the board of directors or the shareholders' meeting in accordance with the provisions of the articles of association;
(2) where the company cannot take advantage of such business opportunity in accordance with the provisions of laws, administrative regulations, or the articles of association.

Article 195 For public issuance of company bonds, the company shall register with the securities regulatory authority of the State Council and announce the method for raising company bonds.
The method for raising company bonds shall specify the following main matters:
(1) the name of the company;
(2) the use of the funds raised from the bonds;
(3) the total amount of the bonds and their face value;
(4) the method for determining the bond interest rate;
(5) the term and method for repayment of principal and payment of interest;
(6) the bond guarantee situation;
(7) the issuing price of the bonds and the start and end dates of issuance;
(8) the net asset value of the company;
(9) the total amount of company bonds already issued but not yet due;
(10) the underwriting institution for the company bonds.

Article 198 Where a company issues company bonds, it shall maintain a register of company bondholders.
For issuance of company bonds, the register of company bondholders shall specify the following matters:
(1) the name or title and domicile of each bondholder;
(2) the date on which the bondholder acquired the bonds and the bond numbers;
(3) the total amount of the bonds, the face value, interest rate, term and method for repayment of principal and payment of interest;
(4) the date of issuance of the bonds.

Article 229 A company shall be dissolved under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The term of operation stipulated in the company's articles of association expires, or any other ground for dissolution stipulated in the articles of association occurs;
(2) A resolution on dissolution is adopted by the shareholders' meeting;
(3) Dissolution is necessary due to the merger or division of the company;
(4) The business license is revoked according to law, or the company is ordered to close down or is revoked;
(5) The People's Court orders the dissolution of the company in accordance with Article 231 of this Law.
Where any ground for dissolution specified in the preceding paragraph occurs, the company shall, within ten days, publish the ground for dissolution on the national enterprise credit information publicity system.

Article 234 During the liquidation period, the liquidation group shall exercise the following powers:
(1) To sort out the company's property and prepare a balance sheet and a detailed list of assets respectively;
(2) To notify creditors and make public announcements;
(3) To handle unfinished business of the company related to the liquidation;
(4) To pay off the taxes owed and the taxes incurred during the liquidation process;
(5) To clear claims and debts;
(6) To distribute the remaining property of the company after the settlement of debts;
(7) To represent the company in participating in civil action activities.

Article 254 Any of the following acts shall be punished by the finance department of the people's government at or above the county level in accordance with the "Accounting Law of the People's Republic of China" and other laws and administrative regulations:
(1) Setting up separate accounting books outside the statutory accounting books;
(2) Providing financial and accounting reports containing false records or concealing material facts.
